   $q$To construct an equilateral triangle on segment $\overline{AB}$, Omar sets the compass to $AB$, draws a circle centered at $A$ and a circle centered at $B$, and labels one crossing point $C$. Which reason shows that triangle $ABC$ is equilateral?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$AC = AB$ as radii of the circle centered at $A$, and $BC = AB$ as radii of the circle centered at $B$","is_correct":true},{"text":"the angles at $A$ and $B$ were each copied as $60^\\circ$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"assumed-angles-were-copied"},{"text":"$CA = CB$, so the triangle has two equal sides","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"proved-only-isosceles"},{"text":"point $C$ lies on segment $\\overline{AB}$ at its midpoint","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misplaced-intersection-point"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$AC = AB$ as radii of the circle centered at $A$, and $BC = AB$ as radii of the circle centered at $B$$q$, $q$Both circles have radius $AB$, so $AC = AB$ and $BC = AB$, which makes all three sides equal. Knowing only $CA = CB$ shows the triangle is isosceles, not equilateral.$q$, 'draft', null)
