   $q$Keisha wants the shortest path from a tree at point $T$ to a straight fence along line $\ell$. Keisha draws an arc centered at $T$ that crosses $\ell$ at $R$ and $S$, then draws arcs of equal width centered at $R$ and $S$ that cross at point $U$ on the other side of $\ell$. What is line $TU$?$q$, $q$[{"text":"the line through $T$ parallel to $\\ell$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confused-with-parallel-construction"},{"text":"the line through $T$ perpendicular to $\\ell$","is_correct":true},{"text":"the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{TU}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"bisected-wrong-segment"},{"text":"the line through $T$ that makes a $45^\\circ$ angle with $\\ell$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thinks-arcs-make-45-degrees"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$the line through $T$ perpendicular to $\ell$$q$, $q$Both $T$ and $U$ are equally far from $R$ and $S$, so line $TU$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\overline{RS}$, which is the perpendicular from $T$ to $\ell$. It is not parallel to $\ell$; it crosses $\ell$ at a right angle.$q$, 'draft', null)
