   $q$To bisect $\angle XYZ$, Amaka draws an arc centered at $Y$ that crosses ray $YX$ at $M$ and ray $YZ$ at $N$. What should Amaka do next?$q$, $q$[{"text":"draw arcs of equal width centered at $X$ and $Z$ that cross inside the angle, then draw a ray from $Y$ through that point","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-original-endpoints"},{"text":"draw a second arc centered at $Y$ with a larger width, then connect $M$ to $N$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"repeated-first-step"},{"text":"draw segment $\\overline{MN}$, then draw the line perpendicular to it through $M$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"perpendicular-at-wrong-point"},{"text":"draw arcs of equal width centered at $M$ and $N$ that cross inside the angle at $K$, then draw ray $YK$","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$draw arcs of equal width centered at $M$ and $N$ that cross inside the angle at $K$, then draw ray $YK$$q$, $q$Points $M$ and $N$ are equally far from $Y$, so arcs centered at them meet at a point $K$ on the bisector, and ray $YK$ splits the angle in half. Points $X$ and $Z$ can be any distance from $Y$, so arcs centered there generally miss the bisector.$q$, 'draft', null)
