   $q$When a compass set to the radius is stepped around a circle, the sixth step lands exactly back on the starting point. Why does this happen?$q$, $q$[{"text":"each step forms an equilateral triangle with the center, so it cuts off a central angle of $60^\\circ$","is_correct":true},{"text":"each step cuts off a central angle of $72^\\circ$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-pentagon-angle"},{"text":"each step cuts off a central angle of $30^\\circ$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"halved-central-angle"},{"text":"each step cuts off a central angle of $45^\\circ$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-octagon-angle"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$each step forms an equilateral triangle with the center, so it cuts off a central angle of $60^\circ$$q$, $q$The two radii and the chord all equal the radius, so each triangle with the center is equilateral with a $60^\circ$ central angle, and $6 \cdot 60^\circ = 360^\circ$. An angle of $72^\circ$ would close after 5 steps, which belongs to a pentagon.$q$, 'draft', null)
