   $q$Point $P$ is not on line $\ell$. Which construction does NOT produce a line through $P$ parallel to $\ell$?$q$, $q$[{"text":"draw a transversal through $P$ meeting $\\ell$ at $Q$, then copy the angle at $Q$ to $P$ in the alternate interior position","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thinks-alternate-interior-copy-fails"},{"text":"draw a transversal through $P$ meeting $\\ell$ at $Q$, then copy the angle at $Q$ to $P$ in the corresponding position","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thinks-corresponding-copy-fails"},{"text":"construct the perpendicular from $P$ to $\\ell$, then construct the perpendicular to that line at $P$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thinks-double-perpendicular-fails"},{"text":"construct the perpendicular from $P$ to $\\ell$ and the perpendicular to that line at $P$, then bisect one of the right angles formed at $P$","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$construct the perpendicular from $P$ to $\ell$ and the perpendicular to that line at $P$, then bisect one of the right angles formed at $P$$q$, $q$Bisecting a right angle at $P$ gives a line that makes a $45^\circ$ angle with the parallel through $P$, so it crosses $\ell$ at $45^\circ$ and is not parallel. The other three all work: congruent corresponding or alternate interior angles, or two lines perpendicular to the same line, give parallel lines.$q$, 'draft', null)
